{
  "title": "Auth: make session expiry explicit on both clients",
  "description": "Web (context/AuthContext.tsx): any 401 sets token = null (65-68, 81-83) and the redirect happens indirectly through ProtectedRoute (App.tsx:21-29); no 'session expired' message, and page state isn't torn down. refreshTags (117-131) has no request id, so a late response for an old token can overwrite tags; overlapping refreshes share tagsLoading. iOS: login isn't atomic. \
Shared/RamekinAPI.swift:262-286 saves or deletes the CF-Access credentials and mutates the global client headers before the request, so a failed login leaves them changed; saveServerURL/saveToken/saveUsername ignore their results (296-298), so a failed keychain write silently returns to the login screen. \
Only generated-client builders post .ramekinAuthExpired (Shared/InsecureSession.swift:46-51); performRequestWithResponse (capture, export, uploadLogs, verifyAuthenticated) and AuthenticatedImageLoader never do. handleAuthExpired (RamekinApp.swift:75-139) doesn't compare tokens, so a late 401 from a request made with the old token logs out a session the user just signed into. \
The share extension treats 401 and 403 as signed out (ShareViewController.swift:329-339); the app only reacts to 401. \
Server: no logout or revoke exists (sessions has no revoked state), and bookmarklet tokens use a 100-year expiry as 'never expires' (auth/db.rs:61-83). \
Want: one auth state per client (signed out / signed in with token / expired) where a 401 only expires the token it was sent with, consistent 401/403 handling, and a 'session expired' message. \
A server logout endpoint is optional; ask before adding it. \
Found in the 2026-11-07 implicit-state-machine audit; line numbers are as of e94669d4ca, so re-check each point before fixing.",
  "status": "open",
